all right the National Football League  has been the recipient of widespread  criticism in the last few years for a  variety of issues involving the safety  of its players and players protesting  during the national anthem to protest  racial injustice along with fans  ditching their cable subscriptions those  stories have led to a steady decline in  TV ratings however the league is still  making money and remains an integral  part of American culture a recent  article in Bloomberg Businessweek  explores the league's existential crisis  and whether it's really a crisis at all  Evan Nobby Williams is a sports business  reporter for Bloomberg News and the  co-anchor of that story and joins us  here to date right writer  it's a co-anchor in there maybe thinking  Joey co-author right when you talk about  a head into film this is it's still  gonna be able to watch football change  the channel right so so okay so there  the NFL's taking this major hit right  everyone acknowledges that ratings are  down tremendously for the league still  making a ton of money how do those two  work together yeah it's kind of what we  call it this existential crisis when  you're King everybody wants to criticize  the king and the NFL is king not just in  sports but entertainment the NFL is the  premier entertainment property in the US  right now and it's very easy to  criticize when Colin Kaepernick takes a  knee it gets criticism from both the  left and the right side of its fans  concussions another big one you know  that is an issue that is concerning both  the people that want to see the game  cleaned up and also the people who love  the violent aspects of the game and all  of that we're seeing play out in ratings  yeah Phil makes all its money it costs  more and more money to for an ad in  during the Superbowl exactly area  networks pay more including CBS a more  and more each time that the rights are  up to to broadcast the games and that's  because it's reaching so many people  there's average 15 million people watch  each NFL game that's down from years  past but as you talk about Digital  Trends people moving towards away from  their traditional cable boxes we're  speaking on a network that didn't exist  five years ago and that's because people  are changing the way they watch  television and the NFL remains the king  of the TV world so can the ratings dip  that is affecting the NFL right now  benefit other sports leagues or even  other companies because  it's as we point out in the introduction  there is there has been some blowback to  the Colin Kapernick and the you know  what some people might term a  controversy but others call justice  where football players are taking a knee  during the playing of the national  anthem to protest racial injustice I  note that CNBC has a report out today  that says Nike stock is at an all-time  high and expected to go even higher even  though there were some who were sort of  saying it was going to suffer because of  their ads yeah sure so in the immediacy  the only sports property that's really  growing its TV audience right now is the  NBA and there's no question that as the  NFL continues to suffer a little bit the  NBA is picking up some market share that  said we're talking about two very  different sized  organizations right the NFL as a 15  billion dollar industry the NBA is an  eight or nine billion dollar league so  you know you're talking still about a  huge gap the NBA certainly is going to  pick up some of the people who may be  turning away from the NFL but the NBA  would certainly love to have the NFL's  viewership drivers so is there any  evidence like the president has asserted  occasionally that the protests during  the national anthem have actually had  any impact on the popularity or the  viewership of the NFL no the simple  answer is no and I have a DejaVu part of  my job where late summer every year the  the Packers the Green Bay Packers  declare their revenue and every year I  get on the call and the Packers  president Mark Murphy he goes well it's  been a good year revenue is up for the  14 15 16 I think this year was the 16th  straight time he said that there was no  evidence other owners I've talked to  haven't seen a direct tie quite yet the  problem with with what had Colin did and  the way the NFL handled it is that you  see groups on both sides that are angry  you know there was a graphic that was  shared on Twitter last year with all the  NFL sponsors saying boycott these  sponsors if you're angry about the  kneeling situation you had people on the  right who were angry that that players  were kneeling at all sharing that  graphic and you had people on the Left  who felt like the ownership and Roger  Goodell were reacting poorly right they  were also sharing the ground right you  know it's the strange bedfellows where  you have people on both ends that are  you know that are upset with the league  and that puts the league in a tough  position but still watching football on  the left  sponsors if they're getting the money  and the advertising dollars I mean you  saw the chart the dollars are going you  know it's a straight line up but I'd be  interested to know what the demographics  are because I suspect that not only are  younger people not tuning in as much  because of folks like Colin Kaepernick  who are raising awareness of a very  important issue and also the safety  issue when it comes to football players  I mean I know that's one of the reasons  that a lot of the people that I'm  friends with who watch football our  entire lives and said you know why I  don't want to contribute to an  organization that is potentially  life-threatening for worry no when I  think about that wasn't you know we  talked about the beginning about it you  know football is a Americana right and  so I think about the parents with young  boys who will not now think of football  as an option as a sport and an  extracurricular activity because of that  and how much it sort of goes against how  we feel about the way we treat each  other as Americans we don't watch them  beat each other up and the ratings dip  is nothing compared to the dip we've  seen in youth participation in rapaho  that's it and the way that applies to  the NFL is up for debate I've talked to  owners who are concerned that people are  playing football less and less at the  youth level I've also talked to owners  who think that youth participation is  not and not necessary for you to love  the NFL very few NFL fans played  football when they were younger and they  still believe that they can get people  who didn't play the game but necessarily  want to watch it as you said there are  certainly people out there who feel  guilty just watching the sport as it is  because they're gaining a better  understanding of what's doing to these  young men who are taking the field that  is something the NFL will have to deal  with and they're doing that I mean  they're making the game they're trying  to make the game safer you know they're  changing rules things that were not a  penalty two or three years ago are a  penalty now and again as we talk about  strange bedfellows there are people that  are upset on that on both sides you know  people want to see the game safer other  people love the violence of the game  they love the NFL because people are  running full-speed and bashing their  heads against each other right and if  that is not allowed maybe the NFL and  this is the big question this is the  existential crisis can the NFL be safe  and also be compelling to watch and I  don't think we've answered that question  yet either way they're still making  money lots of people are still watching  no matter what the  president wins a brochure Evan no V  Williams thank you very much

